dante 1.4.4 libdsocks: non-blocking connects from several threads

Applies on top of dante-connectchild-workers.patch and
dante-ipv6-gateway.patch (and Alpine's ifdev, gaierrval and
dante-no-bindresvport patches).

Two races in the mother (the program's process) that crash or wedge
threaded programs doing non-blocking connects from more than one thread,
or receiving SIGIO on more than one. Reproduced with test/mtstress.c
(8 threads churning connects, short waits, fd reuse): SIGSEGV or SIGABRT
within seconds, e.g. sigio() asserting on dup2(2) EBADF, socks_addaddr()
asserting on a corrupted table entry, and in a real program heap/stack
corruption (musl __stack_chk_fail).

1. The connect-child was created lazily without serialization: several
   threads making their first non-blocking connect at the same time each
   forked one and overwrote sockscf.child_data/child_ack. Replies from the
   other children were never read, their connects never finished, and the
   children were orphaned. A mutex now covers the check and the creation.

2. socks_addrlock() skipped the thread mutex whenever sockscf.state.insignal
   was set (an OpenBSD workaround). That flag is process-wide, so while any
   thread ran sigio() no thread was locked out of the address table, and
   SIGIO can be delivered to (and sigio() run on) several threads at once.
   Now:
   - sigio() runs the handler with the address lock held, so one instance
     runs at a time and other threads stay out of the table meanwhile; an
     instance that waited still drains the replies;
   - socks_addrlock() takes the mutex in the handler too (except on
     OpenBSD), and the mutex is recursive for the functions sigio() calls
     that lock themselves. A thread holding the lock has all signals
     blocked, so the handler never runs on the thread holding it.

Test: test/mtstress.c (via test/run.sh).

